;;; 76-superformula.slat — Motoi Scheme Reference · Chapter 76 · library superformula
;;;
;;; 3 verbs in this library. Read chapter 00-intro.slat first.
;;;
;;; ─── THE ROW RULES (summary — full doctrine in 00-intro.slat) ───
;;;
;;;   Row 1 (HOW · Composition) — the verb written well; 1/3/5 module ladder
;;;   Row 2 (WHEN · Audit)      — operator's problem + this-verb-not-a-sibling
;;;   Row 3 (WHERE ELSE · Dim)  — same shape in 3 substrates (verb/metaphor/math)
;;;   Row 4 (WHY · Proof)       — claim + runnable test + ∀-invariant
;;;   Row 5 (WITH WHAT ELSE)    — chained verbs → named emergent pattern
;;;
;;; Every row carries the three-discipline lens: :eng · :math · :cs.
;;; Every code block RUNS through ./bin/motoi. Made by Alfred Borden.
;;;
;;; The Gielis superformula: r(φ) = ( |cos(mφ/4)/a|^n2 + |sin(mφ/4)/b|^n3 )^(−1/n1).
;;; Sample φ around the circle, convert (r, φ) → (x, y), trace a shape. Six numbers
;;; grow circles, polygons, stars, flowers, leaves, gears, shells. m sets the lobe
;;; count/symmetry; n1,n2,n3 set the pinch; a,b set the scale asymmetry.
;;;   Parameter family (all verified to render):
;;;     m=0                         → circle (constant radius)
;;;     m=4, n1=n2=n3 large (20)    → rounded square
;;;     m=5, n1=n2=n3 small (0.3)   → star / flower
;;;     m=6, big n2,n3 (12)         → gear / snowflake
;;;     m=2, a≠b (a=1,b=3)          → leaf / petal
;;;

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "Library superformula — 3 verbs.")

(chapter :number "76" :library "superformula" :verb-count 3 :training-eligible #t)

;; ═══ 3 verbs in library superformula ═══

(verb
  :name "superformula" :std "Motoi"
  :library "superformula"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(superformula cx cy radius m n1 n2 n3 [a] [b] [steps]) -> (list (x y) ...)"
  :summary "Sample the Gielis superformula around a full turn and return the traced outline as a list of (x y) points — pure, no framebuffer needed."
  :explanation "This is the pure sampler at the bottom of the superformula stack. Given a center (cx cy), a base radius, the symmetry number m, and the three exponents n1/n2/n3 (plus optional scale factors a, b and a sample count steps, defaulting a=1, b=1, steps=180), it walks φ from 0 to nearly 2π, evaluates r(φ) = (|cos(mφ/4)/a|^n2 + |sin(mφ/4)/b|^n3)^(−1/n1) at each step, and maps each polar (r, φ) into a Cartesian point (cx + radius·r·cos φ, cy + radius·r·sin φ). Because it returns DATA and paints nothing, it runs without (set-mode 'sd): you can measure it with length/car/cadr, feed it to (poly ...) or (fill-poly ...) from the draw library, translate or rotate the points yourself, or hand it to any code that consumes a polygon. It is the shape-as-a-value; the stroke and fill verbs are the shape-as-a-paint."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(length (superformula 160 120 40 6 1 7 8))" :note "Default steps is 180, so a six-lobed shape sampled with the default schedule returns 180 points — the count you would hand to poly.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define circle (superformula 160 120 40 0 1 1 1))\n(define star   (superformula 160 120 40 5 0.3 0.3 0.3))\n(list 'circle-pts (length circle) 'star-pts (length star) 'same-count? (= (length circle) (length star)))" :note "m=0 gives a circle, m=5 with small exponents gives a five-point star — different shapes, identical point count, because steps (not m) sets the sampling schedule.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (bbox pts)\n  (let ((xs (map car pts)) (ys (map cadr pts)))\n    (list 'x (apply min xs) (apply max xs) 'y (apply min ys) (apply max ys))))\n(define leaf (superformula 0 0 40 2 1 1.7 1.7 1 3 240))\n(bbox leaf)" :note "Sample an asymmetric (a=1, b=3) leaf at 240 steps and read off its bounding box straight from the point list — pure geometry, no paint.")
  )
  :caveats ("The sampler walks φ over [0, 2π) and does NOT repeat the first point at the end; if a consumer needs an explicitly closed polyline, append (car pts) yourself." "Small exponents (n1 well below 1) produce spiky stars whose points can shoot far past the base radius; the base radius is a scale, not a hard bound." "steps controls smoothness AND cost: too few steps turns a flower into a coarse polygon; too many wastes work on a shape nobody will zoom into." "a and b are scale asymmetries, not offsets — a=0 or b=0 divides by zero inside the formula, so keep them positive.")
  :drawbacks ("Returns raw points only: no styling, no color, no fill rule — you must hand the list to a painter or process it yourself." "The (r, φ) → (x, y) mapping is centered on (cx cy); to rotate the whole shape you post-process the points, the verb has no rotation argument." "Because it is pure math it cannot tell you whether the shape will fit on the canvas — clip or scale before painting large radii near the SD edges.")
  :usecases ("Building a polygon you will feed to (poly ...) or (fill-poly ...) with custom color or fill logic." "Measuring a shape (point count, bounding box, centroid) before deciding how to draw it." "Generating a base shape once, then rotating/scaling copies for a mandala or a field of flowers." "Teaching the polar-function-sampled-on-a-schedule idea with a value you can inspect in the REPL.")
  :related ("superformula-stroke" "superformula-fill" "poly" "fill-poly")
  :learn (:concept "A polar function r(φ) sampled on a fixed schedule and converted to a list of Cartesian points — the data underneath every superformula drawing." :prerequisites ("iota" "map") :progression "Start by measuring the returned list with length and bbox; then feed it to poly to draw it; then post-process the points (rotate, scale, translate) to compose copies into scenes.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "A UI designer building a badge generator wants a five-petal rosette but needs to know its actual on-screen footprint before placing it, because small n1 exponents make the star spikes overshoot the nominal radius. They need the shape as measurable data, not a paint they can only eyeball."
     :program "(let* ((pts (superformula 100 100 30 5 0.3 0.3 0.3))\n       (xs (map car pts)) (ys (map cadr pts))\n       (w (- (apply max xs) (apply min xs)))\n       (h (- (apply max ys) (apply min ys))))\n  (list 'width w 'height h 'points (length pts)))"
     :eng-program "(define (footprint-with-margin cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 margin)\n  (let* ((pts (superformula cx cy r m n1 n2 n3))\n         (xs (map car pts)) (ys (map cadr pts)))\n    (list 'left  (- (apply min xs) margin) 'right (+ (apply max xs) margin)\n          'top   (- (apply min ys) margin) 'bottom (+ (apply max ys) margin))))\n(footprint-with-margin 100 100 30 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 4)"
     :math-program "(define (max-radial-excess cx cy r m n1 n2 n3)\n  (let* ((pts (superformula cx cy r m n1 n2 n3))\n         (dist (lambda (p) (sqrt (+ (expt (- (car p) cx) 2) (expt (- (cadr p) cy) 2))))))\n    (list 'nominal r 'actual-max (apply max (map dist pts)))))\n(max-radial-excess 100 100 30 5 0.3 0.3 0.3)"
     :cs-program "(define (points-ok? pts)\n  (and (list? pts) (> (length pts) 0)\n       (every (lambda (p) (and (list? p) (= (length p) 2))) pts)))\n(points-ok? (superformula 100 100 30 5 0.3 0.3 0.3))"
     :explanation "eng: return a padded placement box the layout engine can reserve. math: measure the true maximum radial reach so 'radius' is understood as a scale not a bound. cs: contract-check that the result is a well-formed list of 2-element points."
     :meta "superformula-stroke and superformula-fill paint the shape but return nothing you can measure — the designer would have to render then read pixels back to find the footprint. superformula hands back the points themselves, so the bounding box is one map away. Reaching for a painting sibling here would force a render-then-inspect loop where a pure measurement suffices.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'radius is a scale, not a clip' — the SRE smoke test is (apply max radial-distances) versus the nominal radius; when they diverge the shape overshoots and the layout box must be measured, not assumed." :math "The point set is {(cx + R·r(φ)·cos φ, cy + R·r(φ)·sin φ) : φ ∈ schedule}; the footprint is the bounding box of that set, and max‖p − center‖ = R·max r(φ)." :cs "Constructor returning a list of records; the audit is a fold to min/max over the coordinate projections — O(steps) with O(1) state."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Evaluate one periodic function of an angle at a fixed set of sample angles, and collect the results into a table you can plot or measure."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "superformula" :code "(superformula 0 0 40 6 1 7 8 1 1 12)")
       (:domain "polar-rose"   :code "(define (rose k n)\n  (map (lambda (i)\n         (let* ((phi (* 2 3.14159 (/ i n)))\n                (r (cos (* k phi))))\n           (list (* r (cos phi)) (* r (sin phi)))))\n       (iota n)))\n(rose 4 12)")
       (:domain "fourier-sample" :code "(define (sample-series coeffs n)\n  (map (lambda (i)\n         (let ((t (* 2 3.14159 (/ i n))))\n           (fold + 0 (map (lambda (c k) (* c (cos (* k t)))) coeffs (iota (length coeffs))))))\n       (iota n)))\n(sample-series '(0 1 0.5) 12)"))
     :explanation "Rung 1: the superformula evaluates r(φ) at 12 scheduled angles and maps each to a Cartesian point. Rung 2: a polar rose r=cos(kφ) evaluates the SAME kind of angular function on the same 12-angle schedule and maps it the same way. Rung 3: a Fourier partial sum evaluates a periodic function at n scheduled times and records the value. All three share the shape — a periodic function of an angle, sampled on a schedule, turned into a discrete table."
     :meta "The transfer works because a superformula IS a polar function, a polar function IS a special periodic function of the angle, and a Fourier series is the general periodic function of an angle. 'Sample a periodic function on an angular schedule' is the substrate-independent shape; the rose and the Fourier sum are the same machine with a different r(φ). Grounds in book-of-math ch-advanced-11 (complex-analysis) as the sampled-periodic-function shape.")
    (:eng "Every angular sampler faces the same resolution-versus-cost knob — steps is n, and n too small aliases the shape while n too large wastes evaluations; the SRE lens is 'pick n from the on-screen size, not from habit'." :math "Discretize a 2π-periodic function on a uniform grid φ_i = 2π·i/n; the reconstruction error is governed by the function's harmonic content (Nyquist on the highest lobe)." :cs "Map over iota then transform each sample; the shape shared by scope traces, wavetable synthesis, and any r=f(θ) plot."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "Radial linearity in the base radius: doubling `radius` doubles every point's displacement from the center, because radius is a pure scalar multiplier on r(φ)."
     :program "(define (near a b) (< (abs (- a b)) 0.0001))\n(define p1 (superformula 0 0 10 4 3 3 3 1 1 8))\n(define p2 (superformula 0 0 20 4 3 3 3 1 1 8))\n(define (scaled? pair)\n  (and (near (* 2 (car (car pair))) (car (cadr pair)))\n       (near (* 2 (cadr (car pair))) (cadr (cadr pair)))))\n(display (scaled? (list (car p1) (car p2)))) (newline)\n(display (scaled? (list (list-ref p1 3) (list-ref p2 3))))"
     :eng-program "(define (scale-invariance-audit cx cy m n1 n2 n3 radii)\n  (let* ((base (superformula cx cy 1 m n1 n2 n3 1 1 8)))\n    (map (lambda (R)\n           (let ((scaled (superformula cx cy R m n1 n2 n3 1 1 8)))\n             (list 'R R 'holds?\n                   (every (lambda (b s)\n                            (< (abs (- (* R (car b)) (car s))) 0.001))\n                          base scaled))))\n         radii)))\n(scale-invariance-audit 0 0 4 3 3 3 '(2 5 10 40))"
     :math-program "(define (near a b) (< (abs (- a b)) 0.0001))\n(define p1 (superformula 0 0 1 6 1 7 8 1 1 16))\n(define p10 (superformula 0 0 10 6 1 7 8 1 1 16))\n(display (every (lambda (a b) (and (near (* 10 (car a)) (car b))\n                                    (near (* 10 (cadr a)) (cadr b))))\n                p1 p10))"
     :cs-program "(define (homothety? cx cy m n1 n2 n3 k)\n  (let ((a (superformula cx cy 1 m n1 n2 n3 1 1 8))\n        (b (superformula cx cy k m n1 n2 n3 1 1 8)))\n    (every (lambda (pa pb)\n             (and (< (abs (- (* k (- (car pa) cx)) (- (car pb) cx))) 0.001)\n                  (< (abs (- (* k (- (cadr pa) cy)) (- (cadr pb) cy))) 0.001)))\n           a b)))\n(homothety? 0 0 4 3 3 3 3.5)"
     :invariant "∀ cx cy m n1 n2 n3 (schedule fixed) ∀ k > 0. point_i(radius=k) − center = k · (point_i(radius=1) − center)  (the shape at radius k is the radius-1 shape scaled about its center by k)"
     :meta "The CS pattern is a homothety — scaling about a fixed center is a linear map on the displacement vectors, and radius is exactly that scale factor sitting outside r(φ). Falsified if doubling radius changed a point's displacement by anything other than a factor of two — that would mean radius interacts with the exponents, contradicting the closed form where it multiplies r(φ) as a constant. book-of-math ch-advanced-04 (groups-symmetry) grounds scaling maps; book-of-code ch 11 (the-alu) grounds distribute-a-scalar-over-a-vector.")
    (:eng "The engineering consequence is that a designer can author a shape at radius 1 and resize it freely by one number without the silhouette drifting; the check re-runs only when the exponents change." :math "The map is p ↦ center + k·(p − center); it commutes with the sampler because R factors out of R·r(φ)·(cos φ, sin φ)." :cs "Scalar-distributes-over-displacement; the same shape as multiplying every element of a vector by a constant."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "superformula returns one shape's outline as a list of points."
     :composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (rot p cx cy ang)\n  (let* ((dx (- (car p) cx)) (dy (- (cadr p) cy))\n         (c (cos ang)) (s (sin ang)))\n    (list (+ cx (- (* dx c) (* dy s))) (+ cy (+ (* dx s) (* dy c))))))\n(define petal (superformula 160 120 34 5 0.4 0.4 0.4))\n(for-each\n  (lambda (i)\n    (poly (map (lambda (p) (rot p 160 120 (* i (/ 3.14159 6)))) petal)\n          (+ 1 (modulo i 14))))\n  (iota 12))"
     :eng-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (grid-of-shapes cols rows spacing r m n1 n2 n3)\n  (for-each\n    (lambda (i)\n      (let ((cx (+ 40 (* spacing (modulo i cols))))\n            (cy (+ 40 (* spacing (quotient i cols)))))\n        (poly (superformula cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 1 1 90)\n              (+ 9 (modulo i 6)))))\n    (iota (* cols rows))))\n(grid-of-shapes 3 3 80 22 5 0.3 0.3 0.3)"
     :math-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (concentric center rmin rmax step m n1 n2 n3)\n  (for-each\n    (lambda (r) (poly (superformula center center r m n1 n2 n3 1 1 120)\n                      (modulo r 15)))\n    (let loop ((r rmin) (acc '()))\n      (if (> r rmax) (reverse acc) (loop (+ r step) (cons r acc))))))\n(concentric 120 12 48 12 6 1 7 8)"
     :cs-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define shapes (list (list 5 0.3 0.3 0.3) (list 6 1 7 8) (list 4 20 20 20)))\n(for-each\n  (lambda (spec i)\n    (let ((cx (+ 60 (* 100 i))))\n      (poly (apply superformula cx 120 30 (append spec (list 1 1 100)))\n            (+ 2 (* 3 i)))))\n  shapes (iota 3))"
     :emergent "A rotational mandala: one petal is sampled once, then twelve rotated copies of the same point list are painted around a shared center, producing a symmetric rosette the model has never seen as a primitive — it emerged from one shape plus a rotation fold."
     :meta "This is the orbit-under-a-symmetry-group family — take one motif and apply every element of a rotation group to it, and the union of the images is a symmetric figure. Familiar analogs are kaleidoscopes (reflect a wedge), tiling by a wallpaper group, and phyllotaxis (rotate a seed by the golden angle). The composition works because superformula is a pure value (Row 4's homothety and the shared schedule mean every rotated copy is congruent), so mapping a rotation over the points and painting each copy accumulates a mandala. Grounds in book-of-math ch-advanced-04 (groups-symmetry) as the orbit-of-a-motif shape.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'sample once, transform many' — the SRE win is that the expensive trig sampling runs a single time and the fold only applies cheap affine maps, so a 24-fold mandala costs one superformula call plus 24 rotations, not 24 samplings." :math "The figure is the union of g·motif over g in the cyclic group C_k; each g is a rotation by 2πj/k about the center." :cs "Map a group action over a base point list, reduce by painting; the shape of every instanced-geometry renderer that reuses one mesh under many transforms."))
)

(verb
  :name "superformula-stroke" :std "Motoi"
  :library "superformula"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(superformula-stroke cx cy radius m n1 n2 n3 [a] [b] [steps] [color]) -> undefined"
  :summary "Paint the OUTLINE of a superformula shape onto the SD framebuffer — the sampler wired straight to a polyline stroke. Requires (set-mode 'sd)."
  :explanation "This is the outline painter of the stack. It runs the same Gielis sampler as the pure superformula verb (same defaults: a=1, b=1, steps=180) but instead of handing back points it connects them edge to edge and strokes the closed contour in the given color (a palette index 0–15 or a quoted color name; omit it to use the current color). Because it writes pixels it needs a framebuffer, so any block that calls it must begin with (set-mode 'sd). Reach for it when you want the SILHOUETTE — a wireframe flower, the rim of a gear, the boundary of a leaf — and do not care about the interior. It is the one-liner that saves you from calling superformula and poly yourself; the tradeoff is you give up access to the intermediate points."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(superformula-stroke 160 120 40 6 1 7 8)" :note "A six-lobed snowflake outline centered on the SD canvas, drawn in the current color with the default 180-step schedule.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(superformula-stroke 160 120 50 4 20 20 20 1 1 180 'cyan)\n(superformula-stroke 160 120 30 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 180 'yellow)" :note "Layer two outlines: a rounded square (m=4, big equal exponents) in cyan, a five-point star (m=5, small exponents) in yellow, sharing a center.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(for-each\n  (lambda (m) (superformula-stroke 160 120 40 m 1 7 8 1 1 120 (+ 1 m)))\n  (list 3 4 5 6 7))" :note "Sweep the symmetry number m from 3 to 7 at a fixed center, each outline a different palette color — a family portrait of how m grows the lobe count.")
  )
  :caveats ("Requires (set-mode 'sd) first; calling it with no framebuffer has nothing to paint on." "It strokes but does not fill — the interior stays whatever was underneath; use superformula-fill for a solid shape." "Very spiky shapes (tiny n1) can send spikes past the canvas edge; the stroke clips at the framebuffer bounds rather than scaling to fit." "The color argument is last and optional; if you pass fewer than the full arg list you get the a/b/steps defaults, so to set color you must also state a, b, and steps.")
  :drawbacks ("Returns undefined — you cannot measure the shape it drew; if you need the points as well, call superformula and stroke the result yourself." "One color per call: a two-tone outline requires two calls or post-processing." "No line-width control here — the outline is a single-pixel polyline; thick rims mean multiple concentric strokes.")
  :usecases ("Wireframe previews of a shape before committing to a fill." "Rims and borders — the edge of a gear, the outline of a leaf, a badge frame." "Overlaying several shapes' silhouettes to compare their extents." "Animating a growing outline by re-stroking with an increasing radius each frame.")
  :related ("superformula" "superformula-fill" "poly" "ngon")
  :learn (:concept "Painting a closed polyline directly from the superformula sampler — the outline half of shape rendering." :prerequisites ("set-mode" "superformula") :progression "Draw one outline; then layer several with different m or radius; then pair it with superformula-fill to get a filled shape with a contrasting rim.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "A game artist prototyping enemy sprites wants quick wireframe silhouettes to compare shapes at a glance — a spiky star for a hazard, a rounded blob for a friendly. They want the rim only, drawn in one call, without threading a point list through a separate polygon painter."
     :program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(superformula-stroke 80 120 30 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 120 'red)\n(superformula-stroke 200 120 30 8 1 1 1 1 1 120 'green)"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (silhouette-row specs y r)\n  (for-each\n    (lambda (spec i)\n      (apply superformula-stroke (+ 60 (* 100 i)) y r\n             (append spec (list 1 1 100 (+ 2 (* 4 i))))))\n    specs (iota (length specs))))\n(silhouette-row (list (list 5 0.3 0.3 0.3) (list 8 1 1 1)) 120 28)"
     :math-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (stroke-and-measure cx cy r m n1 n2 n3)\n  (let* ((pts (superformula cx cy r m n1 n2 n3))\n         (xs (map car pts)))\n    (superformula-stroke cx cy r m n1 n2 n3)\n    (list 'span (- (apply max xs) (apply min xs)))))\n(stroke-and-measure 160 120 30 5 0.3 0.3 0.3)"
     :cs-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (guarded-stroke cx cy r m n1 n2 n3)\n  (if (and (number? r) (> r 0) (integer? m) (> n1 0))\n      (begin (superformula-stroke cx cy r m n1 n2 n3) 'drawn)\n      'rejected-bad-params))\n(guarded-stroke 160 120 30 5 0.3 0.3 0.3)"
     :explanation "eng: a reusable row-of-silhouettes helper that fans specs across x. math: sample first to measure the span, then stroke — the outline and the measurement agree because both come from the same sampler. cs: precondition guard on radius/m/n1 before any pixels are touched."
     :meta "poly could draw the same outline, but only after the artist calls superformula to get points — two calls where one suffices. ngon draws regular polygons only and cannot make a spiky star or an asymmetric leaf. superformula-stroke is the sampler-and-stroke fused into a single silhouette primitive; a sibling either needs a helper feed or cannot express the shape family.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'this call paints and returns nothing' — the SRE smoke test is not a return value but a framebuffer diff; the artist confirms the shape appeared, and the only failure mode worth logging is a missing (set-mode 'sd)." :math "The stroke is the image of the sampled point set under the polyline map — edges between consecutive samples, closing the last to the first, over the schedule φ ∈ [0, 2π)." :cs "Fused sample-then-rasterize; O(steps) trig evaluations plus O(steps) line segments, no intermediate list retained by the caller."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Trace the boundary of a region by walking its edge as a sequence of connected line segments."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "superformula-stroke" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(superformula-stroke 160 120 40 6 1 7 8)")
       (:domain "contour-walk" :code "(define (perimeter-length pts)\n  (let loop ((ps pts) (first (car pts)) (total 0))\n    (cond ((null? (cdr ps))\n           (+ total (sqrt (+ (expt (- (car first) (car (car ps))) 2)\n                             (expt (- (cadr first) (cadr (car ps))) 2)))))\n          (else\n           (let ((a (car ps)) (b (cadr ps)))\n             (loop (cdr ps) first\n                   (+ total (sqrt (+ (expt (- (car b) (car a)) 2)\n                                     (expt (- (cadr b) (cadr a)) 2))))))))))\n(perimeter-length (superformula 0 0 40 6 1 7 8 1 1 24))")
       (:domain "closed-curve-integral" :code "(define (signed-area pts)\n  (let loop ((ps pts) (first (car pts)) (acc 0))\n    (if (null? (cdr ps))\n        (/ (abs (+ acc (- (* (car (car ps)) (cadr first))\n                          (* (car first) (cadr (car ps)))))) 2)\n        (let ((a (car ps)) (b (cadr ps)))\n          (loop (cdr ps) first\n                (+ acc (- (* (car a) (cadr b)) (* (car b) (cadr a)))))))))\n(signed-area (superformula 0 0 40 4 20 20 20 1 1 60))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1: the stroke walks the sampled boundary and paints a segment between each consecutive pair, closing the loop. Rung 2: a perimeter measurement walks the SAME boundary and sums the segment lengths instead of painting them. Rung 3: the shoelace area integral walks the same boundary and accumulates a cross-product instead. All three walk one closed polyline edge by edge; only the accumulator differs — pixels, length, or area."
     :meta "The transfer works because a stroked outline, a perimeter, and a shoelace area are three folds over the same edge sequence — paint, add-lengths, add-cross-products. 'Walk the closed boundary as consecutive segments' is the substrate-independent shape; the discrete boundary walk is the numeric cousin of a line integral around a closed curve. Grounds in book-of-math ch-advanced-11 (complex-analysis) as the closed-contour-traversal shape.")
    (:eng "Every boundary-walk faces the same closure question — does the last segment return to the first vertex? — and the SRE lens is 'a broken outline is an unclosed walk'; the stroke closes implicitly, a measurement must close explicitly." :math "A closed polyline is a cyclic sequence of vertices; traversing consecutive pairs (with wrap-around) visits every edge exactly once. Green's theorem relates the boundary walk to the enclosed area." :cs "Fold over adjacent pairs of a cyclic list; the shape shared by perimeter, area, and polyline rasterization."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "Color-independence of geometry: the shape stroked does not depend on the color argument — changing only the color leaves the same silhouette, because color is styling applied after the sampler fixes the geometry."
     :program "(define geom-a (superformula 160 120 40 6 1 7 8 1 1 90))\n(define geom-b (superformula 160 120 40 6 1 7 8 1 1 90))\n(display (equal? geom-a geom-b)) (newline)\n(set-mode 'sd)\n(superformula-stroke 160 120 40 6 1 7 8 1 1 90 'red)\n(superformula-stroke 160 120 40 6 1 7 8 1 1 90 'blue)\n(display 'both-strokes-same-geometry)"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (color-sweep-audit cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 colors)\n  (let ((geom (superformula cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 1 1 90)))\n    (for-each (lambda (col) (superformula-stroke cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 1 1 90 col)) colors)\n    (list 'geometry-fixed-points (length geom) 'colors-drawn (length colors))))\n(color-sweep-audit 160 120 40 6 1 7 8 (list 'red 'green 'blue))"
     :math-program "(define (near a b) (< (abs (- a b)) 0.0001))\n(define g1 (superformula 0 0 40 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 60))\n(define g2 (superformula 0 0 40 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 60))\n(display (every (lambda (a b) (and (near (car a) (car b)) (near (cadr a) (cadr b)))) g1 g2))"
     :cs-program "(define (geometry-hash cx cy r m n1 n2 n3)\n  (length (superformula cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 1 1 90)))\n(define h1 (geometry-hash 160 120 40 6 1 7 8))\n(define h2 (geometry-hash 160 120 40 6 1 7 8))\n(list 'stable? (= h1 h2))"
     :invariant "∀ cx cy radius m n1 n2 n3 a b steps ∀ c1 c2. geometry(stroke(...,c1)) = geometry(stroke(...,c2))  (the traced contour is a function of the shape parameters alone; color changes only the pixels' hue)"
     :meta "The CS pattern is separation of geometry from style — the sampler is a pure function of the shape parameters, and color is a late-bound rendering attribute that cannot perturb it. Falsified if swapping red for blue moved a single vertex — that would mean color leaks into the sampler, breaking the layer boundary between 'what shape' and 'what color'. book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) grounds the pure-function-of-its-inputs idea; book-of-math ch-advanced-04 grounds the invariance-under-a-relabeling shape.")
    (:eng "The engineering consequence is that a theming pass can recolor every outline without any chance of the layout shifting; geometry review and color review are independent tickets." :math "The stroke factors as render ∘ sample, where sample depends on (cx…steps) and render binds a color; the two axes are orthogonal." :cs "Style-independent-geometry; the same shape as a widget whose layout is invariant under its CSS color."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "superformula-stroke paints one shape's outline in one color."
     :composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (nested-rings cx cy rmin rmax step m n1 n2 n3)\n  (for-each\n    (lambda (r) (superformula-stroke cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 1 1 120 (modulo r 15)))\n    (let loop ((r rmin) (acc '()))\n      (if (> r rmax) (reverse acc) (loop (+ r step) (cons r acc))))))\n(nested-rings 160 120 12 52 10 6 1 7 8)"
     :eng-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (badge cx cy)\n  (superformula-stroke cx cy 50 4 20 20 20 1 1 140 'cyan)\n  (superformula-stroke cx cy 40 5 0.4 0.4 0.4 1 1 140 'yellow)\n  (superformula-stroke cx cy 18 0 1 1 1 1 1 140 'white))\n(badge 160 120)"
     :math-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (rose-of-strokes cx cy r k)\n  (for-each\n    (lambda (i) (superformula-stroke cx cy r (+ 2 i) 1 7 8 1 1 100 (+ 1 i)))\n    (iota k)))\n(rose-of-strokes 160 120 40 6)"
     :cs-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (stroke-catalog specs cx cy r steps)\n  (for-each\n    (lambda (spec i)\n      (apply superformula-stroke cx cy r (append spec (list 1 1 steps (+ 1 (modulo i 15))))))\n    specs (iota (length specs))))\n(stroke-catalog (list (list 3 1 7 8) (list 5 0.3 0.3 0.3) (list 6 1 12 12)) 160 120 40 90)"
     :emergent "A concentric target / mandala frame: the same silhouette is stroked at growing radii in cycling colors, and the nested rings read as a single ringed emblem — a rose window built purely from outlines, no fill, no imported asset."
     :meta "This is the level-set / contour-family shape — one shape function drawn at a family of scales produces nested boundaries, like the contour lines on a topographic map or the equipotential rings of a field. Familiar analogs are onion-skinning in animation, tree rings, and iso-lines in scientific visualization. The composition works because superformula-stroke is scale-driven by its radius argument (Row 4's geometry is color-independent, and the pure sampler's Row 4 shows radius is a clean homothety), so stroking across a radius schedule yields congruent nested contours. Grounds in book-of-math ch-advanced-15 (dynamical-systems-chaos-fractals) as the level-set-family shape.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'each ring is an independent paint with no shared state', so the loop is trivially reorderable and the only cost is steps × ring-count line segments; the SRE knob is the radius step, which trades density for fill time." :math "The family {stroke(shape, R) : R ∈ schedule} is a one-parameter family of level curves of the same shape function, nested by the monotone radius map." :cs "Map a paint over a scale schedule; the shape of every contour-plot renderer and every onion-skin frame stack."))
)

(verb
  :name "superformula-fill" :std "Motoi"
  :library "superformula"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(superformula-fill cx cy radius m n1 n2 n3 [a] [b] [steps] [color]) -> undefined"
  :summary "Paint a SOLID superformula shape onto the SD framebuffer — the sampler wired to a filled polygon. Requires (set-mode 'sd)."
  :explanation "This is the solid painter of the stack. It samples the same Gielis contour (defaults a=1, b=1, steps=180) and then fills the interior of the closed shape with the given color (palette index 0–15 or a quoted name; omit for the current color). Like the stroke verb it writes pixels, so its block must open with (set-mode 'sd). Reach for it when you want a MASS, not a rim — a filled flower petal, a solid gear body, a leaf you can stack into foliage. It differs from superformula-stroke by covering the interior with the scan-fill rule rather than tracing only the edge, and from the pure superformula by painting instead of returning points. It is how a superformula shape becomes an opaque object a scene can be built from."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(superformula-fill 160 120 40 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 180 11)" :note "A solid five-point star (m=5, small equal exponents) filled with palette color 11 — the classic superformula star as a mass.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(superformula-fill 160 120 45 6 1 12 12 1 1 180 7)\n(superformula-fill 160 120 20 0 1 1 1 1 1 180 14)" :note "A filled gear (m=6, big n2/n3) with a solid hub disc (m=0) painted on top — layering fills to build a two-part object.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(for-each\n  (lambda (i)\n    (superformula-fill (+ 60 (* 100 (modulo i 3)))\n                       (+ 60 (* 80 (quotient i 3)))\n                       22 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 120 (+ 9 (modulo i 6))))\n  (iota 6))" :note "A field of six filled stars laid on a 3×2 grid, each in a cycling color — solid shapes as scene tiles.")
  )
  :caveats ("Requires (set-mode 'sd); with no framebuffer there is nothing to fill." "Self-intersecting shapes (extreme exponents) can leave the scan-fill ambiguous where the contour crosses itself; verify visually before trusting the coverage." "Fill paints over whatever is beneath — order matters, so paint back-to-front when layering." "Like the stroke verb the color argument is last; to set it you must also supply a, b, and steps.")
  :drawbacks ("Returns undefined — no measurement of the shape it painted; sample with superformula first if you need the geometry too." "Solid single-color coverage only: gradients, textures, or per-region shading require additional passes." "For a shape with both a distinct rim color and a fill you must call superformula-fill then superformula-stroke — this verb does the interior only.")
  :usecases ("Solid game sprites — stars, blobs, gears, petals as opaque objects." "Foliage and flower fields where many filled shapes stack into a scene." "Backgrounds and masks: a filled shape as a clip region for later drawing." "Building a compound object by stacking filled shapes (gear + hub, flower + center).")
  :related ("superformula" "superformula-stroke" "fill-poly" "ellipse-fill")
  :learn (:concept "Filling the interior of a sampled closed shape — the solid half of shape rendering, complementary to the outline stroke." :prerequisites ("set-mode" "superformula" "superformula-stroke") :progression "Fill one solid shape; then stack fills back-to-front to make a compound object; then combine with superformula-stroke to add a contrasting rim over the fill.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "A tile-map designer needs opaque terrain decals — solid flower clusters scattered over a background — and needs them to cover the ground beneath completely so no seam shows through. An outline would let the background bleed through; they need mass, and they need to control paint order so newer flowers sit on top."
     :program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(superformula-fill 100 120 24 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 120 10)\n(superformula-fill 160 130 24 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 120 11)\n(superformula-fill 220 118 24 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 120 12)"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (scatter-flowers placements r)\n  (for-each\n    (lambda (place)\n      (apply superformula-fill (car place) (cadr place) r\n             (list 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 120 (caddr place))))\n    placements))\n(scatter-flowers '((100 120 10) (160 130 11) (220 118 12)) 24)"
     :math-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (fill-with-coverage cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 col)\n  (let* ((pts (superformula cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 1 1 120))\n         (xs (map car pts)) (ys (map cadr pts)))\n    (superformula-fill cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 1 1 120 col)\n    (list 'covers-x (- (apply max xs) (apply min xs))\n          'covers-y (- (apply max ys) (apply min ys)))))\n(fill-with-coverage 160 120 24 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 10)"
     :cs-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (fill-in-bounds cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 col w h)\n  (if (and (>= (- cx r) 0) (>= (- cy r) 0) (<= (+ cx r) w) (<= (+ cy r) h))\n      (begin (superformula-fill cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 1 1 120 col) 'painted)\n      'skipped-would-clip))\n(fill-in-bounds 160 120 24 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 10 320 240)"
     :explanation "eng: a placement-list scatterer that fans filled flowers across the map. math: sample to report the coverage box the decal occupies. cs: an in-bounds guard so a decal that would clip the canvas edge is skipped rather than truncated."
     :meta "superformula-stroke would trace the flower rim and let the ground show through the middle — wrong for an opaque decal. fill-poly could fill it, but only after a separate superformula call to get points. ellipse-fill fills only ellipses and cannot make a five-point star. superformula-fill is the sampler-and-fill fused for exactly this opaque-shape-in-one-call job.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'coverage, not outline' — the SRE smoke test is that the fill occludes the pixels beneath its bounding shape; a decal that lets background bleed through is a fill that silently degraded to a stroke." :math "The painted set is the interior {p : p inside the sampled contour} by the even-odd (or nonzero-winding) rule, not just the boundary." :cs "Fused sample-then-scanfill; O(steps) sampling plus O(area) or O(spans) fill, versus the stroke's O(steps) segments — the fill is the heavier cousin."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Decide, for every point, whether it lies inside a closed region, and mark the inside."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "superformula-fill" :code "(set-mode 'sd)\n(superformula-fill 160 120 40 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 120 11)")
       (:domain "point-in-polygon" :code "(define (inside? pt poly)\n  (let loop ((ps poly) (j (- (length poly) 1)) (i 0) (c #f))\n    (if (>= i (length poly)) c\n        (let ((pi (list-ref poly i)) (pj (list-ref poly j)))\n          (if (and (not (eq? (> (cadr pi) (cadr pt)) (> (cadr pj) (cadr pt))))\n                   (< (car pt) (+ (car pi)\n                                  (/ (* (- (car pj) (car pi)) (- (cadr pt) (cadr pi)))\n                                     (- (cadr pj) (cadr pi))))))\n              (loop (cdr ps) i (+ i 1) (not c))\n              (loop (cdr ps) i (+ i 1) c))))))\n(inside? '(0 0) (superformula 0 0 40 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 40))")
       (:domain "characteristic-function" :code "(define (region-membership pts test)\n  (map (lambda (p) (list p (test p))) pts))\n(region-membership '((0 0) (100 100)) (lambda (p) (< (+ (* (car p) (car p)) (* (cadr p) (cadr p))) 1600)))"))
     :explanation "Rung 1: the fill marks every interior pixel of the sampled shape. Rung 2: a point-in-polygon test answers inside-or-out for one point against the same sampled contour. Rung 3: a characteristic function maps each point to a boolean membership. All three answer 'is this point inside the region?' — the fill answers it for the whole raster, the polygon test for one point, the characteristic function for an arbitrary set."
     :meta "The transfer works because filling is the point-in-polygon test evaluated over the entire pixel grid — the scan-fill is just an efficient batch of the membership query. 'Is a point inside a closed boundary?' is the substrate-independent shape; a set's characteristic function 1_S is the abstract form of a fill. Grounds in book-of-code ch 05 (logic-gates-in-scheme) as the membership-predicate shape.")
    (:eng "Every region-marking substrate faces the same edge-case — points exactly on the boundary — and the SRE lens is 'pick a tie-breaking rule and apply it consistently' so the same seam is not painted twice or skipped." :math "The fill computes the indicator 1_R of the region R bounded by the sampled contour; scan-fill exploits the parity of boundary crossings along each scanline." :cs "Point-in-polygon lifted to a raster; the shape shared by flood-fill, polygon rasterization, and set-membership tests."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "The fill covers the same footprint as the outline of the same parameters: filling and stroking a shape with identical arguments produce coverage over the same bounding box, because both consume one sampler with one schedule."
     :program "(define (bbox pts)\n  (let ((xs (map car pts)) (ys (map cadr pts)))\n    (list (apply min xs) (apply max xs) (apply min ys) (apply max ys))))\n(define geom (superformula 160 120 40 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 120))\n(define box (bbox geom))\n(set-mode 'sd)\n(superformula-fill   160 120 40 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 120 11)\n(superformula-stroke 160 120 40 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 120 'white)\n(display (equal? box (bbox (superformula 160 120 40 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 120))))"
     :eng-program "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (fill-vs-stroke-footprint cx cy r m n1 n2 n3)\n  (let* ((pts (superformula cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 1 1 120))\n         (xs (map car pts)))\n    (superformula-fill   cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 1 1 120 11)\n    (superformula-stroke cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 1 1 120 'white)\n    (list 'shared-span (- (apply max xs) (apply min xs)))))\n(fill-vs-stroke-footprint 160 120 40 5 0.3 0.3 0.3)"
     :math-program "(define (near a b) (< (abs (- a b)) 0.0001))\n(define g (superformula 0 0 40 6 1 7 8 1 1 90))\n(define xs (map car g))\n(display (and (near (apply min xs) (apply min (map car (superformula 0 0 40 6 1 7 8 1 1 90))))\n              (near (apply max xs) (apply max (map car (superformula 0 0 40 6 1 7 8 1 1 90))))))"
     :cs-program "(define (same-footprint? cx cy r m n1 n2 n3)\n  (let ((a (superformula cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 1 1 90))\n        (b (superformula cx cy r m n1 n2 n3 1 1 90)))\n    (equal? (map car a) (map car b))))\n(same-footprint? 160 120 40 5 0.3 0.3 0.3)"
     :invariant "∀ cx cy radius m n1 n2 n3 a b steps. bbox(fill(...)) = bbox(stroke(...)) = bbox(superformula(...))  (fill and stroke share the sampler, so they occupy the identical footprint; they differ only in interior coverage)"
     :meta "The CS pattern is a shared-source guarantee — fill, stroke, and the pure sampler all derive from one function, so their extents cannot disagree; only the interior-versus-boundary decision differs. Falsified if a filled shape spilled past its own stroked outline — that would mean the fill and stroke sample different contours, breaking the promise that the three verbs are three views of one shape. book-of-code ch 05 grounds the same-inputs-same-geometry idea; book-of-math ch-advanced-11 grounds boundary-and-interior of one region.")
    (:eng "The engineering consequence is that a designer can fill a shape and stroke it with the same arguments to get a solid shape with a crisp rim, confident the rim hugs the fill exactly; a mismatch would be a rendering bug, not a parameter mistake." :math "Boundary and interior of one region share the region's bounding box; ∂R ⊆ closure(R) and both have the same convex hull extents." :cs "Two consumers of one generator; the same shape as a min and a max computed from one sorted list agreeing on the list's range."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "superformula-fill paints one solid shape."
     :composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (flower-with-center cx cy r petal-col center-col)\n  (superformula-fill cx cy r 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 120 petal-col)\n  (superformula-fill cx cy (quotient r 3) 0 1 1 1 1 1 120 center-col))\n(flower-with-center 160 120 40 11 14)"
     :eng-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (meadow rows cols spacing r)\n  (for-each\n    (lambda (i)\n      (let ((cx (+ 40 (* spacing (modulo i cols))))\n            (cy (+ 40 (* spacing (quotient i cols)))))\n        (superformula-fill cx cy r 5 0.3 0.3 0.3 1 1 100 (+ 9 (modulo i 6)))\n        (superformula-fill cx cy (quotient r 3) 0 1 1 1 1 1 100 14)))\n    (iota (* rows cols))))\n(meadow 2 3 90 22)"
     :math-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (stacked-scales cx cy)\n  (for-each\n    (lambda (r) (superformula-fill cx cy r 2 1 1.7 1.7 1 3 120 (modulo r 15)))\n    (list 40 30 20 10)))\n(stacked-scales 160 120)"
     :cs-composition "(set-mode 'sd)\n(define (compound-gear cx cy)\n  (superformula-fill cx cy 45 6 1 12 12 1 1 140 7)\n  (superformula-fill cx cy 26 0 1 1 1 1 1 140 8)\n  (superformula-fill cx cy 8  0 1 1 1 1 1 140 0))\n(compound-gear 160 120)"
     :emergent "A flower-field scene: each cell fills a solid petal-star and stamps a solid center disc on top, and the grid of them reads as a meadow of flowers — a compound object stacked from filled superformula shapes, painted back-to-front so each center lands cleanly on its petals."
     :meta "This is the painter's-algorithm / layered-fill family — build a compound object by painting solid regions back-to-front so nearer masses occlude farther ones. Familiar analogs are cel-based cartoon compositing, sprite-stacking in isometric games, and the way a snowman is three discs stacked bottom-up. The composition works because superformula-fill covers its footprint opaquely (Row 4 pins that footprint to the shared sampler, and Row 2 confirms coverage occludes), so each later fill safely sits on the earlier ones. Grounds in book-of-code ch 14 (assembly-then-higher) as the layered-composition shape, and book-of-sim-games (scene-graph) as the painter's-order shape.")
    (:eng "The audit lens is 'paint order is the correctness contract' — the SRE fix for a center that vanishes is 'you painted it before the petals', so the build must fill background masses first; total cost is per-fill scanfill summed over the layer count." :math "The final image is the ordered overwrite fold ⨁ of the layer fills, where later layers override earlier ones on shared pixels — a last-writer-wins reduction over the region stack." :cs "Painter's algorithm; the same back-to-front overwrite that scene graphs, window compositors, and layered raster editors all use."))
)
